\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An exact matter-weighted sum on the three-by-three torus}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take the [square torus](fdip-006M) with both periods three,
#{G=\{1,-1\}}, real scalar matter, unit edge weights, #{m^2=1}
and bare coupling #{\beta=0}. Vertices are #{(x,y)} with coordinates
in #{\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z}; use horizontal edges #{a_{x,y}} and
vertical edges #{b_{x,y}} in the orientations of that definition.
There are nine matter coordinates and eighteen links.}

\p{Fix a spanning tree by requiring
##{a_{0,y}=a_{1,y}=1\quad(y=0,1,2),\qquad
 b_{0,0}=b_{0,1}=1.}
These eight edges connect every vertex without a cycle. Parameterize
the ten remaining links by #{s=(s_1,\ldots,s_{10})\in\{1,-1\}^{10}}:
##{\begin{aligned}
(a_{2,0},a_{2,1},a_{2,2})&=(s_1,s_2,s_3),\\
(b_{1,0},b_{2,0},b_{1,1},b_{2,1})&=(s_4,s_5,s_6,s_7),\\
(b_{0,2},b_{1,2},b_{2,2})&=(s_8,s_9,s_{10}).
\end{aligned}}
Let #{U_e(s)} denote these assigned signs.}

\p{In the vertex order
#{(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(0,1),\ldots,(2,2)}, let #{E_{v,w}}
be a matrix unit and define
##{\begin{aligned}
H_s&=\sum_{e\in E}U_e(s)(E_{h(e),s(e)}+E_{s(e),h(e)}),\\
d_s&=\det(5I-H_s),\\
P_{x,y}(s)&=a_{x,y}b_{x+1,y}a_{x,y+1}b_{x,y},\\
k(s)&=\#\{(x,y):P_{x,y}(s)=-1\}.
\end{aligned}}
The symbol #{s(e)} in a matrix index is the edge's source vertex;
the tuple #{s} labels the ten free signs. All subscripts on links
are read modulo three. Every #{d_s} is a positive integer.}

\p{For each connection there is a unique gauge transformation with
value one at #{(0,0)} that makes the tree links positive: propagate
its vertex values along the tree. The only remaining transformations
fixing a connection are the two constant signs, since the graph is
connected. Hence every gauge orbit has #{2^8} elements and exactly
one displayed representative. Gauge-invariant normalized edge sums
therefore reduce to the following exact formulas:
##{\begin{aligned}
R&=\sum_{s\in\{1,-1\}^{10}}d_s^{-1/2},&
Z_U&=2^{-10}R,\\
\langle P_{0,0}\rangle
 &=\frac{\sum_sP_{0,0}(s)d_s^{-1/2}}{R},&
\mathfrak Z&=(2\pi)^{9/2}Z_U.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{Z_U} is the determinant-weighted edge integral, whereas
#{\mathfrak Z} includes ordinary matter volume.}

\p{The finite sums contain 1024 terms. They can be evaluated by
forming the displayed integer matrix for each sign tuple and taking
its determinant. As checks, the determinants have 43 distinct values,
from 614656 to 967680. At the all-positive tuple, the eigenvalues of
#{5I-H_s} are #{1}, four copies of #{4}, and four copies of #{7},
so #{d_s=4^4 7^4=614656}. Grouping by the number of negative
plaquettes gives the table; probabilities are rounded evaluations of
the exact radical sums.}

##{\begin{array}{c|r|c|c}
k&\text{orbits}&\text{pure gauge }(\beta=0)&\text{with matter}\\\hline
0&4&0.00390625&0.00404317\\
2&144&0.14062500&0.14286686\\
4&504&0.49218750&0.49338270\\
6&336&0.32812500&0.32522204\\
8&36&0.03515625&0.03448524
\end{array}}

\p{Explicitly, the matter probability in row #{k} is
#{R^{-1}\sum_{s:k(s)=k}d_s^{-1/2}}; the pure probability is the
orbit count divided by 1024. The resulting values are
##{Z_U\approx0.00109665722870537,\qquad
 \langle P_{0,0}\rangle\approx0.00300474565684294.}
Translation invariance gives the same expectation at every plaquette.
The [pure-gauge torus law](fdip-006R) at #{\beta=0} instead has
#{\langle P_{0,0}\rangle=0}. No large-mass truncation was used in
these finite sums.}
